counterfactuals.output:
We analyze the effect of nonresonant isovector strong interactions on the Coulomb-modified charged-to-neutral production ratio in near-threshold heavy-meson pair production. For \(B\bar B\) and \(D\bar D\) pairs produced through the predominantly isoscalar resonances \(\Upsilon(4S)\) and \(\psi(3770)\), the usual Coulomb enhancement of the charged channel is modified by strong \(I=1\) final-state scattering. Although this scattering is nonresonant, its interference with the electromagnetic Coulomb amplitude can generate corrections comparable to, or larger than, the nominal Coulomb effect. The resulting ratio
\[
R^{c/n}=\frac{\Gamma(B^+B^-\,\,{\rm or}\,\,D^+D^-)}
{\Gamma(B^0\bar B^0\,\,{\rm or}\,\,D^0\bar D^0)}
\]
therefore depends not only on the meson velocity and the Coulomb factor, but also on the isovector strong phase shift and short-distance production dynamics. We show that this sensitivity limits purely electromagnetic predictions for \(R^{c/n}\) and provides a possible probe of nonresonant \(I=1\) meson–antimeson scattering near threshold. The same framework applies to \(K\bar K\) production at the \(\phi(1020)\), where the smaller kaon velocity enhances Coulomb effects, while strong isovector \(K\bar K\) scattering and the charged–neutral mass splitting can substantially distort the charged/neutral yield ratio. These considerations emphasize that precision extraction of production rates or resonance parameters from charged and neutral meson channels requires a combined treatment of Coulomb interactions and nonresonant strong final-state phases.
